# authors.mailmap
#
# Merges the different names and emails one person commits under into a
# single identity. git_authorship.py applies this file automatically to every
# repository it analyzes, on top of the repository's own .mailmap. Student
# repositories are never modified.
#
# Format (one line per alias; see `git help gitmailmap`):
#
#   Proper Name <proper@email>  <alias@email>
#   Proper Name <proper@email>  Alias Name <alias@email>
#
# The left side is how the person is reported. The right side is the identity
# to replace. Matching is on email, and on name too when a name is given.
#
# To find aliases in a repo:
#   git -C <repo> log --format='%aN <%aE>' | sort | uniq -c
#   git -C <repo> log --format='%(trailers:key=Co-authored-by,valueonly)' | sort -u
#
# Cloud coding agents (Copilot coding agent, Claude on GitHub) name the student
# as <username>@users.noreply.github.com, sometimes as
# <id>+<username>@users.noreply.github.com. Map both forms when in doubt.

# --- Instructor -----------------------------------------------------------
Susan Hammond <sahammond@lipscomb.edu>  <1664398+sah0017@users.noreply.github.com>
Susan Hammond <sahammond@lipscomb.edu>  <sah0017@users.noreply.github.com>

# --- Students -------------------------------------------------------------
# Add entries as aliases are discovered, for example:
# Jane Student <jstudent@mail.lipscomb.edu>  <jstudent-gh@users.noreply.github.com>
# Jane Student <jstudent@mail.lipscomb.edu>  <12345678+jstudent-gh@users.noreply.github.com>
# Jane Student <jstudent@mail.lipscomb.edu>  jstudent-gh <jstudent@mail.lipscomb.edu>
